pepper plants are popular at the museo  de siencias natorales  in madrid spain a country famous for  using peppers in cuisine  yet it's their roots rather than their  succulent fruits  that were the subject of new research  conducted at the museum's greenhouse  though a plant's growth above ground is  highly observable  its subterranean development lacks  visibility  this forces scientists to rely on  piecemeal techniques  to measure roots spatial behavior or  growth patterns according to distance  and limited mathematical models to  predict their biomass  the recent research in madrid sought to  reconcile this dilemma  a new mathematical formula incorporated  both space  and biomass to build a more complete  model of root growth  researchers then tested its accuracy  using a novel experiment  first they set up soil-filled  construction gutters inside a greenhouse  in some gutters they arranged pairs of  pepper plants  roughly 10 centimeters apart in others  solitary plants were grown without  neighbors all of the plants were given  equal access to light and water  as well as fertilizer rich with  phosphorus nitrogen  iron and other key nutrients as the  plants grew  scientists injected the plant stems with  red and blue dyes  so that they could distinguish between  the roots of competing plants  finally they piece together root  fragments according to their owners  measuring their density lengths and  positions in the soil beds  plants tend to invest resources closer  to their stems  adding more roots to the soil near their  source as they  spread symmetrically but the experiment  showed paired plants invested even more  resources closer to their stems  than solitary plants they also grew  fewer roots as they neared their  neighbors  where resources became scarcer amid  greater competition  these patterns closely aligned with the  study's model  which predicted that neighboring plant  roots would segregate and change how  they invest resources  in order to acquire as many resources as  possible  by confirming the model the research  helped resolve  contradictory results from previous  experiments  where root biomass sometimes increased  and other times decreased  in the presence of neighboring plants  turns out the closer the plants are to  their neighbors  the more they invest in their roots  overall  their evolutionary programming causes  them to add  more and more roots near their stems  rather than extend across  space to compete for survival the  researchers believe  their findings could help genetically  reprogram plants to maximize crop yields  climate modelers could also make better  projections of earth's vegetation  the significant carbon sink by factoring  in the underground life of plants  oh the places they'll grow  [Music]  you
 

